" stroke="#D8CBBB" stroke-width="2"/><rect x="344" y="602" width="172" height="44" rx="18" fill="#ECCDA0"/><rect x="344" y="652" width="172" height="44" rx="18" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#D8CBBB" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="360" cy="714" r="6" fill="#6E645C"/><circle cx="500" cy="714" r="6" fill="#6E645C"/><line x1="600" y1="237" x2="660" y2="237" stroke="#C85A32" stroke-width="3"/><circle cx="660" cy="237" r="7" fill="#C85A32"/><rect x="680" y="185" width="460" height="104" rx="14" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#D8CBBB" stroke-width="2"/><rect x="680" y="185" width="10" height="104" rx="4" fill="#C85A32"/><text x="708" y="219" font-size="22" font-weight="700" fill="#342E2A">TOP SHELF<tspan font-size="18" font-weight="400" fill="#C85A32"> · Rarely used</tspan></text><text x="708" y="249" font-size="17" fill="#6E645C">Guest sheets, spare pillows,</text><text x="708" y="273" font-size="17" fill="#6E645C">extra blankets in labeled baskets</text><line x1="600" y1="365" x2="660" y2="365" stroke="#8FA689" stroke-width="3"/><circle cx="660" cy="365" r="7" fill="#8FA689"/><rect x="680" y="313" width="460" height="104" rx="14" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#D8CBBB" stroke-width="2"/><rect x="680" y="313" width="10" height="104" rx="4" fill="#8FA689"/><text x="708" y="347" font-size="22" font-weight="700" fill="#342E2A">EYE LEVEL<tspan font-size="18" font-weight="400" fill="#8FA689"> · Daily use</tspan></text><text x="708" y="377" font-size="17" fill="#6E645C">Bath towels and hand towels</text><text x="708" y="401" font-size="17" fill="#6E645C">for everyone in the home</text><line x1="600" y1="495" x2="660" y2="495" stroke="#B98A5E" stroke-width="3"/><circle cx="660" cy="495" r="7" fill="#B98A5E"/><rect x="680" y="443" width="460" height="104" rx="14" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#D8CBBB" stroke-width="2"/><rect x="680" y="443" width="10" height="104" rx="4" fill="#B98A5E"/><text x="708" y="477" font-size="22" font-weight="700" fill="#342E2A">LOWER SHELF<tspan font-size="18" font-weight="400" fill="#B98A5E"> · Weekly</tspan></text><text x="708" y="507" font-size="17" fill="#6E645C">Sheet sets in their pillowcases,</text><text x="708" y="531" font-size="17" fill="#6E645C">guest and beach towels</text><line x1="600" y1="638" x2="660" y2="638" stroke="#6E645C" stroke-width="3"/><circle cx="660" cy="638" r="7" fill="#6E645C"/><rect x="680" y="586" width="460" height="104" rx="14" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#D8CBBB" stroke-width="2"/><rect x="680" y="586" width="10" height="104" rx="4" fill="#6E645C"/><text x="708" y="620" font-size="22" font-weight="700" fill="#342E2A">FLOOR<tspan font-size="18" font-weight="400" fill="#6E645C"> · Bulky and seasonal</tspan></text><text x="708" y="650" font-size="17" fill="#6E645C">Comforters and heavy blankets</text><text x="708" y="674" font-size="17" fill="#6E645C">in bins or on a rolling cart</text><rect x="60" y="750" width="1080" height="96" rx="14" fill="#8FA689"/><text x="600" y="790" font-size="22" font-weight="700" fill="#FFFFFF" text-anchor="middle">How much to keep</text><text x="600" y="822" font-size="18" fill="#FFFFFF" text-anchor="middle">2 sheet sets per bed  ·  2 towel sets per person  ·  2 extra towel sets for guests</text><text x="600" y="878" font-size="15" fill="#6E645C" text-anchor="middle">Based on linen closet organization advice from Merry Maids.</text></svg><figcaption style="text-align:center;font-size:0.9em;color:#6E645C;">Keep what you use every day at eye level, and move everything else up or down.</figcaption></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The house cleaning experts at <a href="https://merrymaids.com/blog/linen-closet-organization" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Merry Maids</a> suggest a similar layout: spare sheet sets on the higher shelves, everyday bath and hand towels in the middle, and seasonal comforters and heavy blankets in bins at the bottom. They also give a simple rule for how much to keep: two sets of sheets for each bed, two sets of towels for each person, and two extra towel sets for guests. That rule alone frees up a surprising amount of space.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Quick Pick: Linen Closet Ideas by Problem</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Your problem</th><th>Start with these ideas</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Too many linens</td><td>The rule of two (#1), donate old sets (#2)</td></tr><tr><td>Can&#8217;t find matching sheets</td><td>Sheets in pillowcases (#4), label by bed (#5)</td></tr><tr><td>Towel stacks keep falling</td><td>Fold in thirds (#7), shelf dividers (#9)</td></tr><tr><td>Small or narrow closet</td><td>Door organizer (#13), shallow shelves (#14), go vertical (#15)</td></tr><tr><td>No linen closet at all</td><td>Under-bed bins (#16), bedroom storage (#17), bathroom cabinet (#18)</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">How to Organize a Linen Closet in 6 Steps</h2>



<ol class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Empty it completely.</strong> Take everything out so you can see what you really own.</li>



<li><strong>Sort by type.</strong> Make piles for sheets, towels, blankets, pillows and anything else living in there.</li>



<li><strong>Cut down to what you need.</strong> Use the rule of two and let go of stained, torn or mismatched pieces.</li>



<li><strong>Clean the shelves.</strong> Wipe them down and let them dry before anything goes back in.</li>



<li><strong>Put things back by zone.</strong> Follow the shelf map: daily at eye level, rarely used up top, bulky on the floor.</li>



<li><strong>Label and maintain.</strong> Label baskets and shelves, and do a quick reset whenever you change the sheets.</li>
</ol>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">18 Linen Closet Organization Ideas</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">1. Follow the rule of two</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Two sheet sets per bed means one on the bed and one clean in the closet. Two towel sets per person works the same way. Anything beyond that, plus a couple of guest sets, is usually extra.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">2. Donate or repurpose old linens</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Animal shelters often accept old towels and blankets. Worn sheets make great drop cloths, and old towels can become cleaning rags in the garage or laundry room. If your washer lives in a closet nearby, our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/laundry-closet-ideas/">laundry closet ideas</a> help you keep both spaces tidy.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">3. Keep guest linens separate</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Store guest sheets and towels together in one basket on the top shelf. When someone visits, you grab one basket instead of digging through everything.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Sheets and Bedding</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/linen-closet-02-sheet-sets.jpg" alt="Bed sheet sets folded and tucked inside their matching pillowcases with blank tags on a linen closet shelf" class="wp-image-869" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/linen-closet-02-sheet-sets.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/linen-closet-02-sheet-sets-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/linen-closet-02-sheet-sets-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/linen-closet-02-sheet-sets-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">4. Store sheet sets inside their pillowcases</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Fold the fitted sheet, flat sheet and one pillowcase, then tuck them all inside the second pillowcase. Every set stays together, and the stacks look neat without any extra containers. It&#8217;s one of the most useful linen closet ideas you can try today.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">5. Label sets by bed or room</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A small tag on each bundle, like &#8220;Queen, main bedroom&#8221; or &#8220;Twin, kids room&#8221;, means anyone in the family can grab the right set without unfolding three others.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">6. Store comforters and duvets low</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Bulky bedding is heavy and only changes with the seasons. Keep it in a bin or on a rolling cart on the closet floor, where it&#8217;s easy to pull out and doesn&#8217;t crush the shelves.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Towels</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/linen-closet-03-towels.jpg" alt="Linen closet shelf with bath towels folded in thirds, rolled hand towels and washcloths in a woven basket" class="wp-image-867" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/linen-closet-03-towels.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/linen-closet-03-towels-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/linen-closet-03-towels-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/linen-closet-03-towels-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">7. Fold towels in thirds</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Folding bath towels in thirds lengthwise, then in half or thirds again, gives you a clean, even edge that stacks straight and looks tidy from the front.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">8. Roll hand towels and washcloths</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Small towels are easier to grab when they&#8217;re rolled in a row or dropped into a basket. You can pull one out without toppling the rest.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">9. Use shelf dividers</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Clip-on shelf dividers keep tall stacks from leaning into each other. They&#8217;re a cheap linen closet organizer that works especially well on deep wire shelves.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">For more ideas on keeping towels and supplies tidy right where you use them, see our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/bathroom-organization-ideas/">bathroom organization ideas</a>.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Baskets, Bins and Labels</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/linen-closet-04-labeled-baskets.jpg" alt="Labeled woven baskets and clear bins holding spare toiletries, first aid supplies and extra towels in a linen closet" class="wp-image-868" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/linen-closet-04-labeled-baskets.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/linen-closet-04-labeled-baskets-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/linen-closet-04-labeled-baskets-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/linen-closet-04-labeled-baskets-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">10. Use matching baskets on high shelves</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Items stored up high are hard to see. Matching woven baskets turn the top shelf into neat &#8220;drawers&#8221; you can pull down in one move.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">11. Add clear bins for small items</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Spare toiletries, first aid supplies and travel sizes get lost between towels. Clear lidded bins keep them together and let you see what&#8217;s inside at a glance.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">12. Label every basket</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Simple labels make the system easy to keep. When everyone knows where things go, the closet stays organized after laundry day, not just the day you set it up.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Small and Narrow Linen Closets</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/linen-closet-05-narrow-hallway.jpg" alt="Narrow hallway linen closet with shallow shelves of towels and an over-the-door organizer with clear pockets" class="wp-image-865" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/linen-closet-05-narrow-hallway.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/linen-closet-05-narrow-hallway-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/linen-closet-05-narrow-hallway-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/linen-closet-05-narrow-hallway-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">13. Use the back of the door</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">An over-the-door organizer with clear pockets holds small items like cotton pads, extra soap and medicine, freeing up shelf space for towels and sheets.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">14. Choose shallow shelves</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">In a narrow hallway closet, items get pushed to the back of deep shelves and forgotten. Shallow shelves or a single row of stacks keeps everything visible.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">15. Go vertical</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Add a shelf riser or an extra shelf so you&#8217;re not wasting the tall gap above short stacks. More shelves with shorter stacks are easier to use than a few very tall piles.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/linen-closet-06-closet-floor.jpg" alt="Bottom of a linen closet with a rolling cart of folded comforters, a laundry basket, cleaning supplies and a step stool" class="wp-image-866" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/linen-closet-06-closet-floor.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/linen-closet-06-closet-floor-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/linen-closet-06-closet-floor-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/linen-closet-06-closet-floor-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Use the floor too. A rolling cart for comforters and a basket for items waiting to be washed keep the lower shelves clear, and a small step stool makes the top shelf reachable. If your closet also holds clothes, our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/small-closet-organization-ideas/">small closet organization ideas</a> can help you share the space.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">No Linen Closet? Try These</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">16. Under-bed storage</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Flat bins under the bed are perfect for spare sheet sets. You can store each bed&#8217;s sheets right under it.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">17. Bedroom storage</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A dresser drawer, a blanket basket at the foot of the bed or a storage bench can hold extra bedding without needing a dedicated closet.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">18. Bathroom cabinets</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Keep towels in the bathroom where you use them, in a cabinet, on an over-the-toilet shelf or rolled in a basket. Our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/how-to-organize-a-laundry-room/">laundry room organization guide</a> also shows how to set up a folding spot so clean linens go straight back where they belong.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/linen-closet-07-before-after.jpg" alt="Linen closet before and after: messy piles of towels and sheets next to the same closet neatly folded with labeled baskets" class="wp-image-864" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/linen-closet-07-before-after.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/linen-closet-07-before-after-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/linen-closet-07-before-after-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/linen-closet-07-before-after-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The before-and-after above is the same closet. The difference is fewer linens, sheets stored in sets, towels folded the same way and a basket for everything small.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Common Linen Closet Mistakes</h2>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Keeping too many sets.</strong> Extra linens are the main reason closets overflow.</li>



<li><strong>Storing daily towels up high.</strong> If you need a step stool every morning, move them to eye level.</li>



<li><strong>Separating sheets from pillowcases.</strong> Mismatched pieces waste time and space.</li>



<li><strong>Putting linens away damp.</strong> Always make sure towels and sheets are fully dry before storing them.</li>



<li><strong>No labels.</strong> Without them, the system falls apart after the first busy week.</li>
</ul>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Linen Closet Organization FAQ</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">What is the best way to organize a linen closet?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Store items by how often you use them: everyday towels at eye level, spare sheets and guest linens up high, and bulky comforters on the floor. Keep sheet sets together and label baskets.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">How many sheets and towels should you keep?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A common rule is two sheet sets per bed, two towel sets per person and two extra towel sets for guests.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">How do you keep sheet sets together?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Fold the fitted sheet, flat sheet and one pillowcase, then tuck them inside the second pillowcase. The whole set stays in one neat bundle.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">What should you store in a linen closet?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Sheets, towels, blankets and pillows, plus small extras like spare toiletries and first aid supplies in clear bins. Avoid storing things that don&#8217;t belong there, like cleaning chemicals on the same shelf as linens.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Short on space in the bedroom too? See our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/small-bedroom-storage-ideas/">small bedroom storage ideas</a> for storage beds, wall shelves and more.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">In UK homes, linen often lives in the airing cupboard; see our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/airing-cupboard-shelves/">airing cupboard shelves</a> guide.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Final Thoughts</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Good linen closet organization comes down to three things: keep only what you need, store each item by how often you use it, and label everything. Start with one shelf this weekend, and the whole closet will be easier to keep tidy every time you change the sheets.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Save this guide to Pinterest for your next linen closet reset.</p>



